; The standard library ships WITH the binary. Installed into the `share`
; section it lands at <prefix>/share/pol/lib/, the fourth entry of the `(load
; "FILE")` search order in tooling/cli/cli_io.ml — so an installed `pol`, run from
; any directory, resolves (load "stdlib.pol") without $POL_LIB being set.
;
; TWO files, and the second is not .pol: `ct.rules` is a library of RELATIONS
; (interrogator extension §1), loaded by a .rules file rather than a model. It
; resolves by the same search order, because the resolver matches on the NAME
; and never on the extension.
;
; This list is explicit while every other shipping path — the Makefile's two,
; the Dockerfile's, the tarball installer's — copies core/stdlib/* whole. That
; asymmetry is dune's: an install stanza takes files, not globs. A new library
; file therefore ships everywhere EXCEPT opam until it is named here, which is
; the one place to remember.
;
; The stanza lives in the ROOT dune rather than core/stdlib/dune so that
; core/stdlib/ stays what its name says: pol data, and nothing else.

; The `static` profile is what `make release` builds with. A dynamically linked
; binary carries its build machine's glibc version as a floor — a tarball built
; on glibc 2.43 refuses to start on Debian 12 (2.36) — which makes "portable
; release" a false claim. Linking libc in removes the floor entirely: the
; resulting binary runs on any Linux of the same architecture, glibc or musl.
; The only cost is the linker's `dlopen` warning, and `pol` never dlopens
; anything (no Dynlink, no NSS lookups). It needs a static libc (libc.a) on the
; BUILD host; where there is none — macOS has none by design — build the tarball
; with `make release STATIC=0` and accept a same-machine-family binary.
;
; This applies to every directory below the root, which is the point: the CLI
; and the language server both get it.

(env
 (static
  (link_flags
   (:standard -ccopt -static))))

(install
 (section share)
 (package pol)
 (files
  (core/stdlib/stdlib.pol as lib/stdlib.pol)
  (core/stdlib/ct.rules as lib/ct.rules)))
